      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Inverse of a Larger Matrix",
      "problem": "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $AB=BA={I}_{3}$\n\nII. $AC=CA={I}_{3}$\n\nIII. $B={A}^{-1}$",
      "worked_solution": "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false. As a result, $B\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is false too.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $CA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CA & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
